{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>You shall not remove the boundary.</b> From the language of ״נָסֹגוּ אָחוֹר״ (Isaiah 42) — one who moves the land-division marker backward into his fellow's field, in order to widen his own. But was it not already stated ״לֹא תִגְזֹל״ (Leviticus 19)? What does it teach by saying 'you shall not remove'? It teaches about one who uproots his fellow's boundary that he transgresses two prohibitions. Could this apply even outside the Land? Scripture says <b>in your inheritance that you shall inherit, etc.</b> — in the land of Israel he transgresses two prohibitions, and outside the Land he transgresses only on account of ״לֹא תִגְזֹל״ (Sifrei)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:47:42.063187429Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}